course: dictionary
lesson: 8740
title: Animal words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: You can learn a new word by reading the words around it. These are its context, and they give clues. The sentence «Tigers have white spots on the backs of their ears, which may help cubs follow their mother or look like eyes watching; servals and many other wild cats have them too.» tells you that a serval is a wild cat, like a tiger.
explain: Look for clue words in the same sentence or the one before it. Here you can spot "wild cats", so a serval is an animal, not a person or a plant. Ask yourself: what kind of thing would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Pictures in your head help too. A serval has long legs, very large ears and spotty fur, so you can build a clear picture of it. Words like "grasslands" and "rodents" also tell you where it lives and what it eats.
fact: A serval is a slim wild cat that lives on African grasslands.
fact: A serval has long legs, very large ears and a golden coat with black spots and stripes.
fact: A serval leaps high to catch birds and rodents.
fact: Its big ears help it hear rodents underground.
fact: A serval is a wild animal, not a pet. Some people keep servals illegally, and wildlife groups warn that this is bad for the animal.
exercise: What is a serval? | A type of grass | A small dog with a short tail | A bird that lives in trees | *A slim wild cat from African grasslands
exercise: A serval leaps high. What does it leap up to catch? | *Birds and rodents | Plants and flowers | Tall trees | Fish from rivers
exercise: Which feature helps a serval hear rodents underground? | Its long legs | Its golden coat | Its short tail | *Its very large ears
test: Which of these is a true fact about a serval? | It has short legs for digging | *It is a slim wild cat with a golden coat, black spots and stripes | It lives in Arctic snow | It is a pet that lives happily in homes
test: Why do wildlife groups warn people about keeping servals? | Because they only eat grass | Because they are too small to see | *Because keeping them illegally is bad for the animal | Because they cannot leap at all
exam: In the sentence «Tigers have white spots on the backs of their ears ... servals and many other wild cats have them too», what does "them" refer to? | Rodents | *White spots on the backs of their ears | African grasslands | Tigers only
